- name: blocks_vector_source_x_0_0
  id: blocks_vector_source_x
  parameters:
    affinity: ''
    alias: ''
    comment: 'Postamble

      A postamble is a known bit sequence added at the end of a packet to indicate
      that the packet has finished and to help the receiver cleanly detect the end
      of the transmission.'
    maxoutbuf: '0'
    minoutbuf: '0'
    repeat: 'True'
    tags: '[]'
    type: byte
    vector: '[0xc0, 0xaf]'
    vlen: '1'
  states:
    bus_sink: false
    bus_source: false
    bus_structure: null
    coordinate: [1304, 1476.0]
    rotation: 0
    state: enabled
- name: digital_constellation_decoder_cb_0
  id: digital_constellation_decoder_cb
  parameters:
    affinity: ''
    alias: ''
    comment: This block receives the phase corrected complex IQ signal from the Costas
      Loop. After the Costas Loop reduces carrier phase and small frequency errors,
      the Constellation Decoder decides which QPSK constellation point each received
      symbol is closest to. It then converts these QPSK symbols back into digital
      symbol or bit information. In this setup, the decoder uses the QPSK constellation
      object, so the incoming signal is expected to contain QPSK symbols. The output
      is no longer a complex IQ signal. It is decoded digital data that can be used
      by the following packet and data recovery blocks.
    constellation: qpsk
    maxoutbuf: '0'
    minoutbuf: '0'
  states:
    bus_sink: false
    bus_source: false
    bus_structure: null
    coordinate: [952, 3040.0]
    rotation: 180
    state: disabled
- name: digital_constellation_modulator_0
  id: digital_constellation_modulator
  parameters:
    affinity: ''
    alias: ''
    comment: Constellation Modulator converts input bits into complex IQ symbols according
      to the selected modulation scheme.
    constellation: qpsk
    differential: 'True'
    excess_bw: excess_bw
    log: 'False'
    maxoutbuf: '0'
    minoutbuf: '0'
    samples_per_symbol: sps
    truncate: 'False'
    verbose: 'False'
  states:
    bus_sink: false
    bus_source: false
    bus_structure: null
    coordinate: [288, 1932.0]
    rotation: 0
    state: enabled
- name: digital_correlate_access_code_xx_ts_0
  id: digital_correlate_access_code_xx_ts
  parameters:
    access_code: access_key
    affinity: ''
    alias: ''
    comment: This block searches the received byte stream for the access code. When
      the access code is detected, it marks the start of the packet and adds a packet_len
      tag. This helps the following blocks understand where the packet begins and
      how it should be processed. The threshold value allows a small number of bit
      errors during access code detection.
    maxoutbuf: '0'
    minoutbuf: '0'
    tagname: packet_len
    threshold: '1'
    type: byte
  states:
    bus_sink: false
    bus_source: false
    bus_structure: null
    coordinate: [528, 3556.0]
    rotation: 0
    state: disabled
- name: digital_costas_loop_cc_0
  id: digital_costas_loop_cc
  parameters:
    affinity: ''
    alias: ''
    comment: This block is used for carrier recovery. The received signal may have
      phase and frequency errors because the transmitter and receiver are not perfectly
      synchronized. The Costas Loop tracks and corrects these phase errors so the
      constellation points rotate back to their correct positions. This makes the
      received symbols easier to detect and decode. In this setup, the loop bandwidth
      controls how fast the block reacts to phase changes. The order value should
      match the modulation type, such as 2 for BPSK or 4 for QPSK. This block mainly
      corrects carrier phase and small frequency offsets before symbol decisions are
      made.
    maxoutbuf: '0'
    minoutbuf: '0'
    order: arity
    use_snr: 'False'
    w: phase_bw
  states:
    bus_sink: false
    bus_source: false
    bus_structure: null
    coordinate: [1584, 2936.0]
    rotation: 180
    state: disabled
- name: digital_crc32_bb_0
  id: digital_crc32_bb
  parameters:
    affinity: ''
    alias: ''
    check: 'False'
    comment: This blocks adds CRC bytes to each packet so that the receiver can check
      whether the packet is corrupted or not.
    lengthtagname: length_tag_key
    maxoutbuf: '0'
    minoutbuf: '0'
    packed: 'True'
  states:
    bus_sink: false
    bus_source: false
    bus_structure: null
    coordinate: [1200, 1300.0]
    rotation: 0
    state: true
- name: digital_crc32_bb_0_0
  id: digital_crc32_bb
  parameters:
    affinity: ''
    alias: ''
    check: 'True'
    comment: This block checks the CRC32 value of each received packet. It uses the
      packet_len tag to know the packet boundaries. If the calculated CRC matches
      the received CRC, the packet is accepted. If the CRC does not match, the packet
      is considered corrupted and can be rejected. This is used to detect broken or
      damaged packets at the receiver side.
    lengthtagname: '"packet_len"'
    maxoutbuf: '0'
    minoutbuf: '0'
    packed: 'True'
  states:
    bus_sink: false
    bus_source: false
    bus_structure: null
    coordinate: [1168, 3388.0]
    rotation: 0
    state: disabled
- name: digital_diff_decoder_bb_0
  id: digital_diff_decoder_bb
  parameters:
    affinity: ''
    alias: ''
    coding: digital.DIFF_DIFFERENTIAL
    comment: This block receives the decoded QPSK symbols after the Constellation
      Decoder. Since differential encoding was used at the transmitter side, this
      block performs differential decoding at the receiver side. It recovers the original
      symbol information by looking at the change between consecutive symbols instead
      of only their absolute values. The modulus is set to 4 because QPSK has 4 possible
      constellation states. This helps solve phase ambiguity problems that may occur
      during carrier recovery. After this block, the signal is closer to the original
      transmitted digital data and can be passed to the following packet recovery
      blocks.
    maxoutbuf: '0'
    minoutbuf: '0'
    modulus: '4'
  states:
    bus_sink: false
    bus_source: false
    bus_structure: null
    coordinate: [632, 3044.0]
    rotation: 180
    state: disabled
- name: digital_linear_equalizer_0
  id: digital_linear_equalizer
  parameters:
    adapt_after_training: 'True'
    affinity: ''
    alg: variable_adaptive_algorithm_0
    alias: ''
    comment: This block is used to reduce the distortion caused by the communication
      channel. The received signal may be affected by multipath, reflections, noise,
      or channel imperfections. The Linear Equalizer tries to correct these effects
      by filtering the signal before symbol decisions are made. It uses multiple filter
      taps to compensate for channel distortion. In this setup, the equalizer works
      with 2 samples per symbol and uses an adaptive algorithm to update its filter
      coefficients. The corr_est tag indicates where the training or correction process
      should start. After equalization, the output signal becomes cleaner and easier
      for the demodulation blocks to decode correctly.
    maxoutbuf: '0'
    minoutbuf: '0'
    num_taps: '15'
    sps: '2'
    training_sequence: '[ ]'
    training_start_tag: corr_est
  states:
    bus_sink: false
    bus_source: false
    bus_structure: null
    coordinate: [1448, 2592.0]
    rotation: 0
    state: disabled
- name: digital_map_bb_0
  id: digital_map_bb
  parameters:
    affinity: ''
    alias: ''
    comment: This block maps the decoded symbol values to the expected output values.
      Since the map is [0, 1, 2, 3], the symbols stay in the same order and no real
      remapping is done.
    map: '[0,1,2,3]'
    maxoutbuf: '0'
    minoutbuf: '0'
  states:
    bus_sink: false
    bus_source: false
    bus_structure: null
    coordinate: [416, 3104.0]
    rotation: 180
    state: disabled
- name: digital_pfb_clock_sync_xxx_0
  id: digital_pfb_clock_sync_xxx
  parameters:
    affinity: ''
    alias: ''
    comment: This block takes the complex IQ signal from the USRP Source and performs
      timing synchronization. Its main job is to find the correct time to read each
      received symbol. The received signal may not be perfectly aligned because the
      transmitter and receiver clocks are not exactly the same. This block corrects
      small timing differences and helps the receiver sample each symbol at the right
      moment. It also uses RRC filter taps to make the signal cleaner before demodulation.
      The output is still a complex IQ signal, but it is better synchronized and easier
      for the next demodulation blocks to decode. This block is mainly used for symbol
      timing synchronization, not for frequency correction.
    filter_size: nfilts
    init_phase: nfilts/2
    loop_bw: timing_loop_bw
    max_dev: '1.5'
    maxoutbuf: '0'
    minoutbuf: '0'
    osps: '2'
    sps: sps
    taps: rrc_taps
    type: ccf
  states:
    bus_sink: false
    bus_source: false
    bus_structure: null
    coordinate: [904, 2600.0]
    rotation: 0
    state: disabled
- name: digital_protocol_formatter_bb_0
  id: digital_protocol_formatter_bb
  parameters:
    affinity: ''
    alias: ''
    comment: This block create the packet header, which contains information such
      as the access code and payload length.
    format: hdr_format
    len_tag_key: length_tag_key
    maxoutbuf: '0'
    minoutbuf: '0'
  states:
    bus_sink: false
    bus_source: false
    bus_structure: null
    coordinate: [1496, 1244.0]
    rotation: 0
    state: enabled
- name: network_udp_sink_0
  id: network_udp_sink
  parameters:
    addr: 127.0.0.1
    affinity: ''
    alias: ''
    comment: This block sends the received byte data from GNU Radio to an external
      program using UDP. In this setup, it sends the data to 127.0.0.1 on port 5001,
      which means the data is sent to another application running on the same computer.
      The UDP Packet Data Size defines the maximum size of each UDP packet. This block
      is usually used at the receiver side to forward recovered data, such as video
      or file data, to another program for display or saving.
    header: '0'
    payloadsize: '1472'
    port: '5001'
    send_eof: 'False'
    type: byte
    vlen: '1'
  states:
    bus_sink: false
    bus_source: false
    bus_structure: null
    coordinate: [1648, 3320.0]
    rotation: 0
    state: disabled
- name: network_udp_source_0
  id: network_udp_source
  parameters:
    affinity: ''
    alias: ''
    comment: 'The UDP Source block listens on a specified UDP port.

      It receives UDP packets coming from an external program, another device, or
      a network source.

      It extracts the byte data carried inside each UDP packet.

      It feeds this data into the GNU Radio flowgraph as a byte stream.

      In ourr case, it listens on port 5000 and outputs data with byte type.

      The UDP Packet Data Size parameter defines the maximum amount of data expected
      from each UDP packet.'
    header: '0'
    ipv6: 'False'
    maxoutbuf: '0'
    minoutbuf: '0'
    notify_missed: 'False'
    payloadsize: '1472'
    port: '5000'
    src_zeros: 'False'
    type: byte
    vlen: '1'
  states:
    bus_sink: false
    bus_source: false
    bus_structure: null
    coordinate: [112, 1488.0]
    rotation: 0
    state: disabled
- name: note_0
  id: note
  parameters:
    alias: ''
    comment: "BIG PICTURE - WHAT IS THIS?\nThis is a radio that sends a DIGITAL FILE\
      \ (e.g. a text file) over the air\nusing QPSK. The radio is software (SDR);\
      \ the USRP box is just the antenna\nfront-end. Carrier frequency: 869.525 MHz.\n\
      \nFlow:  file bytes -> packet -> QPSK signal -> antenna\n       -> antenna ->\
      \ recover signal -> bytes -> saved file\nTOP = TRANSMITTER (send),   BOTTOM\
      \ = RECEIVER (recover).\nQPSK = 2 bits per symbol.  sps=4 (4 samples per symbol).\
      \  samp_rate=1 MHz."
    note: BIG PICTURE - WHAT IS THIS?
  states:
    bus_sink: false
    bus_source: false
    bus_structure: null
    coordinate: [1144, 72.0]
    rotation: 0
    state: enabled
- name: note_0_0
  id: note
  parameters:
    alias: ''
    comment: ''
    note: Receiver Side Flow
  states:
    bus_sink: false
    bus_source: false
    bus_structure: null
    coordinate: [232, 2480.0]
    rotation: 0
    state: disabled
- name: note_0_0_0
  id: note
  parameters:
    alias: ''
    comment: ''
    note: Transmitter Side Flow
  states:
    bus_sink: false
    bus_source: false
    bus_structure: null
    coordinate: [112, 1232.0]
    rotation: 0
    state: enabled
- name: note_1
  id: note
  parameters:
    alias: ''
    comment: 'THE SLIDERS (appear as tabs when the program runs)

      TX Baseband Gain : digital amplitude of the signal before the radio

      Transmit Gain    : the radio''s transmit power

      Receive Gain     : the radio''s receive amplification

      Time: BW         : speed of the symbol-timing lock (receiver)

      Phase: Bandwidth : speed of carrier phase/frequency correction (receiver)

      Equalizer: rate  : how fast the equalizer adapts to channel distortion'
    note: THE SLIDERS
  states:
    bus_sink: false
    bus_source: false
    bus_structure: null
    coordinate: [1680, 80.0]
    rotation: 0
    state: enabled
- name: note_2
  id: note
  parameters:
    alias: ''
    comment: "TRANSMITTER - STEP 1: BUILD THE PACKET (data -> bytes -> packet)\n1.\
      \ File / UDP source: brings in the bytes to send.\n2. Stream to Tagged Stream:\
      \ cuts the bytes into packets (packet_len=90)\n   and tags each packet's length.\n\
      3. CRC32 (add): appends an error-check code so the receiver can tell\n   whether\
      \ a packet arrived corrupted.\n4. Protocol Formatter: builds a HEADER (access\
      \ code + length) in front.\n5. Preamble & Postamble: known byte patterns added\
      \ before/after, so the\n   receiver can find where each packet starts and ends.\n\
      6. Tagged Stream Mux: glues PREAMBLE + HEADER + DATA + POSTAMBLE into\n   one\
      \ complete packet."
    note: 'TRANSMITTER - STEP 1: BUILD THE PACKET'
  states:
    bus_sink: false
    bus_source: false
    bus_structure: null
    coordinate: [2288, 72.0]
    rotation: 0
    state: enabled
- name: note_2_0
  id: note
  parameters:
    alias: ''
    comment: "USAGE NOTES FOR FILE, IMAGE, AND VIDEO TRANSFER\nTo send a TXT file\
      \ or an image, use the File Source and File Sink blocks, and disable the UDP\
      \ blocks.\nMake sure the correct file path is specified in the File Source block\
      \ for the file that will be transmitted.\nAlso specify the correct output path\
      \ in the File Sink block on the receiver side.\nTo send video, use the UDP blocks,\
      \ and disable the File Source and File Sink blocks.\nThis is the same for simulation\
      \ as well.\nFor the transmitter side, use only the upper side of the flowgraph,\
      \ up to the USRP Sink block, and disable the bottom side of the flowgraph.\n\
      For the receiver side, do the opposite.\nThrottle, Prob Rate, Message Debug,\
      \ Noise Source, Virtual Sink (stream ID: \"transmit\"), and Virtual Source (stream\
      \ ID: \"transmit\") blocks are only for simulation.\nKeep them disabled during\
      \ real tests with USRP.\nWhen doing simulation only, enable both the transmitter\
      \ and receiver sides.\nEnable Throttle, Prob Rate, Message Debug, Noise Source,\_\
      and the Virtual Sink and Virtual Source blocks that have the stream ID: \"transmit\"\
      .\nDisable the UHD blocks during simulation.\nFor video transfer, if you are\
      \ on the receiver side, run the RX Python file from the Linux terminal and from\
      \ its original directory, not from an arbitrary location.\nThen run the receiver-side\
      \ flowgraph.\nIf you are on the transmitter side, run the flowgraph first.\n\
      Then run the TX Python file from the terminal and from the original directory\
      \ of the TX file.\nMake sure your computer camera is open and not disabled by\
      \ the camera key on the keyboard.\nThe UHD block addresses on both the receiver\
      \ and transmitter sides must be selected according to the SDR devices being\
      \ used.\n2-channel USRP X310 address: \"addr=192.168.10.2\"\n1-channel USRP\
      \ Ettus X310 address: \"addr=192.168.10.3\"\nIf you see 4 points on the constellation\
      \ diagram, it means the signal is being received.\nHowever, if no data is flowing\
      \ in the \"data to write rx\" section, the constellation diagram is not being\
      \ interpreted correctly.\nThis usually means there is a phase shift.\nTo fix\
      \ this, reduce the Phase: Bandwidth parameter using the slider.\nThen adjust\
      \ it until the constellation diagram reaches the correct position.\nWhen the\
      \ correct position is found, the bytes will pass through the CRC32 check and\
      \ will be written to the output file.\nIf the 2 USRP devices are too close to\
      \ each other, do not use too much transmit power."
    note: USAGE NOTES
  states:
    bus_sink: false
    bus_source: false
    bus_structure: null
    coordinate: [88, 520.0]
    rotation: 0
    state: enabled
- name: note_3
  id: note
  parameters:
    alias: ''
    comment: "TRANSMITTER - STEP 2: TURN BITS INTO A SIGNAL\n1. Constellation Modulator\
      \ (QPSK): maps the bits onto QPSK symbols and\n   shapes them into a smooth\
      \ waveform (RRC, excess_bw=0.35).\n2. Tag Gate: removes stream tags so they\
      \ don't disturb the hardware.\n3. x TX Baseband Gain: sets the signal's amplitude.\n\
      4. USRP Sink: sends it out through the antenna.\nThe Time Sink shows the transmitted\
      \ samples on screen."
    note: 'TRANSMITTER - STEP 2: TURN BITS INTO A SIGNAL'
  states:
    bus_sink: false
    bus_source: false
    bus_structure: null
    coordinate: [2304, 392.0]
    rotation: 0
    state: enabled
- name: note_4
  id: note
  parameters:
    alias: ''
    comment: 'SIMULATION CHANNEL (the "sim" part - usually optional/disabled)

      Noise Source + Add: takes the transmitted signal and adds random noise

      to FAKE a real radio channel. This lets you test the receiver WITHOUT

      real hardware: the noisy signal is fed straight back into the receiver.

      More noise = harder for the receiver = more errors.'
    note: SIMULATION CHANNEL
  states:
    bus_sink: false
    bus_source: false
    bus_structure: null
    coordinate: [2312, 608.0]
    rotation: 0
    state: enabled
- name: note_5
  id: note
  parameters:
    alias: ''
    comment: "RECEIVER - RECOVER THE DATA (antenna -> bytes)\n1. USRP Source: receives\
      \ the RF signal.\n2. PFB Clock Sync: matched filter + finds the right instant\
      \ to sample\n   each symbol (symbol timing).\n3. Linear Equalizer (CMA): undoes\
      \ the distortion the channel added.\n4. Costas Loop: corrects carrier phase/frequency\
      \ offset.\n   (The Constellation sink shows 4 clean dots when this works.)\n\
      5. Constellation Decoder + Diff Decoder + Map: symbols back into bits.\n6. Correlate\
      \ Access Code: locates the packet start using the access code.\n7. Repack Bits\
      \ -> CRC32 (check): rebuild bytes, drop corrupted packets.\n8. File / UDP Sink:\
      \ writes the recovered data out."
    note: RECEIVER - RECOVER THE DATA
  states:
    bus_sink: false
    bus_source: false
    bus_structure: null
    coordinate: [2312, 800.0]
    rotation: 0
    state: enabled
